f 17592186436178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436218 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436218 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436218 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186436178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436218 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e132532a-be97-5775-bb17-2406d8caeef2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436219 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436219 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436219 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436219 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436219 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436219 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436219 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436219 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186436205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436219 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e674252e-02a4-53ae-adde-553bbd0c47c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436221 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436221 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436221 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436221 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436221 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436221 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436221 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436221 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186436173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436221 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "efcaebab-808b-586a-b686-8d0602967092" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436224 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436224 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143872" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436224 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436224 #edb/ref 1071 "A company manufactures electronic components. Of the component population, $45%$ are produced by machine $A,$  $25%$ by machine $B,$ and $30%$ by machine $C.$ If $5%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ and $3%$ of those produced by machine $B$ are defective, and the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is $0.042,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected component produced by machine $C$ is defective?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436224 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "199cacb7-9e7a-5d07-af35-fc181af78213" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436224 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly selected component is defective, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation. \n\n- Since machine $A$ produces $45%$ of the components, machine $B$ produces $25%$ of the components, and machine $C$ produces $30%$ of the components, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.45,\\,P(B)=0.25,\\,P(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $5%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ and $3%$ of the components produced by machine $B$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.05,\\,P(D|B)=0.03\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is $0.042,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.042.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.042 & =(0.05)(0.45)+(0.03)(0.25)+P(D|C)(0.3) \\\\ 0.042 & =0.0225+0.0075+0.3P(D|C) \\\\ 0.3P(D|C) & =0.012 \\\\ P(D|C) & =0.04\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected component produced by machine $C$ is defective is $0.04.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436225 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436225 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143748" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436225 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436225 #edb/ref 1071 "A company produces replacement batteries for a particular cell phone model at three locations. Location $A$ produces $30%$ of the batteries, of which $3%$ are defective. Location $B$ produces $38%$ of the batteries, of which $5%$ are defective. And location $C$  produces $32%$ of the batteries, of which also $5%$ are defective. What is the probability that a randomly selected battery is defective?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436225 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "296a9d4d-087e-5552-b718-aa436d5fbac8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436225 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $D$ be the event that the battery is defective, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the battery was produced at location $A,$\n\n- let $B$  be the event that the battery was produced at location $B,$\n\n- let $C$  be the event that the battery was produced at location $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected battery is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since  location $A$ produces $30%$ of the batteries,  location $B$ produces $38%$ of the batteries, and location $C$ produces $32%$ of the batteries, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.3,\\,P(B)=0.38,\\,P(C)=0.32.\n$$\n\n- Since $3%$ of the batteries produced at location $A$, $5%$ of the batteries produced at location $B$, and $5%$ of the batteries produced at  location $C,$ are defective, we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.03,\\,P(D|B)=0.05,\\,P(D|C)=0.05.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected battery is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(0.03)(0.3)+(0.05)(0.38)+(0.05)(0.32) \\\\  & =0.009+0.019+0.016 \\\\  & =0.044.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436228 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436228 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143753" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436228 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436228 #edb/ref 1071 "In a gas station, $40%$ of the clients buy Regular gasoline, $45%$ buy Premium gasoline, and $15%$ buy Supreme gasoline. If $65%$ of the clients that purchase Regular gasoline paid with a credit card, $50%$ of the clients that buy Premium gasoline paid with a credit card, and $60%$ of the clients that buy Supreme gasoline paid with a credit card,  what is the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436228 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7c588063-b6d3-5525-ae01-ed797a4d9cc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436228 #edb/ref 1074 "Let  $C$ be the event that the client paid with a credit card, and\n\n- let $R$ be the event that the client bought Regular gasoline,\n\n- let $Q$  be the event that the client bought Premium gasoline,\n\n- let $S$ be the event that the client bought Supreme gasoline.\n\nThen the disjoint union $R⊔Q⊔S$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C\\cap R)+P(C\\cap Q)+P(C\\cap S) \\\\  & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|Q)P(Q)+P(C|S)P(S)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $40%$ of the clients buy Regular gasoline, $45%$ of the clients buy Premium gasoline, and $15%$ of the clients buy Supreme gasoline, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.4,\\,P(Q)=0.45,\\,P(S)=0.15.\n$$\n\n- Since $65%$ of the clients that buy Regular gasoline, $50%$ of the clients that buy Premium gasoline, and $60%$ of the clients that buy Supreme gasoline,  paid with a credit card, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|R)=0.65,\\,P(C|Q)=0.5,\\,P(C|S)=0.6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|Q)P(Q)+P(C|S)P(S) \\\\  & =(0.65)(0.4)+(0.5)(0.45)+(0.6)(0.15) \\\\  & =0.26+0.225+0.009 \\\\  & =0.575.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492215 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186492947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492215 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186492953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492215 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186492970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492215 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186492977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492215 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186492981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492215 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492215 #edb/ref 1045 "Extending the Law of Total Probability" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492215 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186492953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492215 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492215 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492215 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "cb177df5-72c8-5d78-b659-9b78f111f63f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492225 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{20}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492225 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 1759218604